  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q27-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "27",
    "prompt": "Show that $N\\in\\mathbb N$ can be expressed as the difference of perfect squares $N=a^2-b^2$ (where $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$) if and only if $N$ is odd or congruent to 0 modulo 4.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Exactly the odd natural numbers and the multiples of 4.\n\nFor odd $N$, use $a=\\frac{(N+1)}{2}$, $b=\\frac{(N-1)}{2}$. For $N=4k$, use $a=k+1$, $b=k-1$.",
      "worked": "Suppose $N=a^2-b^2$. Then\n$$N=(a+b)(a-b).$$\nThe factors $a+b$ and $a-b$ have the same parity. If both are odd, $N$ is odd; if both are even, $4\\mid N$.\nConversely, if $N$ is odd, $\\frac{(N+1)}{2}$ and $\\frac{(N-1)}{2}$ are integers, and\n$$N=\\left(\\frac{N+1}{2}\\right)^2-\\left(\\frac{N-1}{2}\\right)^2.$$\nIf $N\\equiv0\\pmod4$, write $N=4k$ with $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(k+1)^2-(k-1)^2&=4k\\\\(k+1)^2-(k-1)^2&=N.\\end{align*}$$\nThus every odd $N$ and every multiple of 4 has the required representation, proving both directions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
